Given the existing SturmZeroCount, a two-sided theorem: if k ≥ K then zeros of any Jacobi solution are spaced ≤ π/√K apart (upper bound on distance between consecutive zeros); if k ≤ K then ≥ π/√K. This is the classical Sturm comparison/oscillation. The D12 SturmComparison file presumably has a suitable engine (`sturm_zero_comparison`). A two-sided result would strengthen U3. But the child task `L4-child-jacobi-zero-interlacing` was already written for it (awaiting dispatch) — duplicating it myself may conflict with the child. Better to leave it to the child and pick something else, or write a *different* piece: e.g. the "conjugate point bound" sharpness.

